    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the length of \\( QS \\), we start by noting that \\( \\angle SQR = 90^\\circ \\) since \\( RS \\) is perpendicular to \\( SQ \\). We need to determine the lengths of \\( SQ \\) and \\( QR \\).\n\nGiven:\n\\[\n\\cos R = \\frac{4}{9}\n\\]\n\nThis implies that:\n\\[\n\\cos R = \\frac{QH}{QR} = \\frac{4}{9}\n\\]\n\nHere, \\( QH \\) represents the length \\( QS \\) and \\( QR \\) represents the length \\( RS \\). Since \\( QS = QR \\sin R \\), we can set up the following relationship:\n\\[\nQS = \\sin R \\cdot RS = \\sin R \\cdot 9\n\\]\n\nNow we need to find \\( \\sin R \\). Using the Pythagorean identity:\n\\[\n(\\sin R)^2 + (\\cos R)^2 = 1\n\\]\n\nSubstitute \\(\\cos R = \\frac{4}{9}\\) into the identity:\n\\[\n(\\sin R)^2 + \\left( \\frac{4}{9} \\right)^2 = 1\n\\]\n\nSolve for \\(\\sin R\\):\n\\[\n(\\sin R)^2 + \\frac{16}{81} = 1\n\\]\n\\[\n(\\sin R)^2 = 1 - \\frac{16}{81}\n\\]\n\\[\n(\\sin R)^2 = \\frac{81}{81} - \\frac{16}{81}\n\\]\n\\[\n(\\sin R)^2 = \\frac{65}{81}\n\\]\n\nTake the square root:\n\\[\n\\sin R = \\sqrt{\\frac{65}{81}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{65}}{9}\n\\]\n\nSubstitute \\(\\sin R = \\frac{\\sqrt{65}}{9}\\) into the relationship \\( QS = \\sin R \\cdot RS \\):\n\\[\nQS = \\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{65}}{9} \\right) \\cdot 9 = \\sqrt{65}\n\\]\n\nThus, the length of \\( QS \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\sqrt{65}}\n\\]",
