      "text": "Given that the cosine of angle $R$ is $\\frac{4}{9}$, and considering that $SQ$ is adjacent to angle $R$, we can apply the definition of cosine using the Pythagorean theorem in triangle $EQR$:\n\n\\begin{eqnarray*}\n\\cos R &=& \\frac{EQ}{ER} \\\\\n\\frac{4}{9} &=& \\frac{eq}{er} \\\\\neq &=& \\frac{4}{7}\\end{eqnarray*}\n\nNext, since both $QS$ and $SQ$ are the lengths of the legs in right triangle $SQR$ and angle $R$ is complementary to angle $QSR$ (by definition of complementary angles), the Pythagorean theorem gives us:\n\n\\begin{eqnarray*}\ncos R &=& \\frac{9}{QSR} \\\\\nQSR &=& 90 - R \\\\\nQSR &=& \\arccos(\\frac{4}{9}) \\\\\nQSR &=& 72.15^\\circ \\\\\nQS &=& \\sqrt{(QS)^2 + (QR)^2} \\\\\nQS_ &=& \\sqrt{ (9/2)^2 + (4/2)^2 } \\\\\nQS &=& \\boxed{5}\n\\end{eqnarray*}",
